1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,169
If the first blockade failed,

2
00:00:03,336 --> 00:00:09,677
why should we expect the second blockade 
to succeed? What's changed here?

3
00:00:09,710 --> 00:00:14,514
The West is leading
Ukraine down the primrose path

4
00:00:14,514 --> 00:00:18,651
the Israelis
are executing a genocide in Gaza.

5
00:00:18,718 --> 00:00:22,389
It's not China's fault
that it wants to be powerful.

6
00:00:22,422 --> 00:00:26,693
So if you get into trouble
and you dial 911,

7
00:00:26,693 --> 00:00:31,064
there's nobody at the other end. 

8
00:00:31,097 --> 00:00:35,268
Hi, this is John Mearsheimer.
Today is August 17th, 2026,

9
00:00:35,268 --> 00:00:41,441
and today is the day that the Memorandum
of understanding that was signed by Iran

10
00:00:41,441 --> 00:00:46,079
and the United States on June
17th expires.

11
00:00:46,212 --> 00:00:50,150
The original memorandum of understanding
called

12
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:53,987
for 60 days of negotiation,
and it was assumed

13
00:00:53,987 --> 00:00:57,624
that at the end of those 60 days,
there would be an agreement.

14
00:00:57,791 --> 00:00:59,592
And at that point the

15
00:01:00,593 --> 00:01:02,429
the memorandum would expire.

16
00:01:02,429 --> 00:01:07,467
Well, 60 days have gone by
and there's obviously been no agreement,

17
00:01:07,467 --> 00:01:12,572
but the memorandum of understanding
has definitely expired,

18
00:01:12,639 --> 00:01:17,143
and there's no effort
being made to extend it.

19
00:01:17,143 --> 00:01:21,281
So the question is,
where are we today in the absence

20
00:01:21,281 --> 00:01:23,249
of the memorandum of understanding?

21
00:01:23,249 --> 00:01:27,120
And it seems quite clear
that what has happened

22
00:01:27,153 --> 00:01:32,559
is that the United States has decided
that it's going to depend on its naval

23
00:01:32,592 --> 00:01:37,097
blockade of Iran to achieve its goals.

24
00:01:37,130 --> 00:01:41,234
So what I'd like to do
today is just talk about

25
00:01:42,335 --> 00:01:45,171
what a naval blockade today

26
00:01:45,171 --> 00:01:50,009
is likely to be able to achieve
for the United States,

27
00:01:50,076 --> 00:01:53,079
given that we don't have any prospect of

28
00:01:53,346 --> 00:01:59,052
a new memorandum of understanding,
what can blockade do

29
00:01:59,052 --> 00:02:00,253
for the United States?

30
00:02:00,253 --> 00:02:04,557
Is it a viable alternative
for getting our way?

31
00:02:04,657 --> 00:02:09,095
Now, the best way to start is just talk
about what our goals are,

32
00:02:09,629 --> 00:02:12,832
and then talk about what
Iran's goals are.

33
00:02:12,899 --> 00:02:15,935
And you want to remember that
when we talk about Iran's goals,

34
00:02:15,935 --> 00:02:19,706
we want to talk about the United States
thwarting Iran's goals.

35
00:02:19,706 --> 00:02:23,910
So the United States, in a very important
way, has two sets of goals here.

36
00:02:23,910 --> 00:02:28,114
One is to cheat, to achieve its own goals,
and number two,

37
00:02:28,181 --> 00:02:32,819
to thwart Iran from achieving its goals.

38
00:02:32,852 --> 00:02:35,889
So let me start by talking
about what America's goals are

39
00:02:35,955 --> 00:02:39,359
and then look at Iran's goals.

40
00:02:39,359 --> 00:02:43,296
And then we can talk about
how well suited blockade is at this point

41
00:02:43,296 --> 00:02:48,735
in time for us, the United States,
achieving its goals.

42
00:02:49,068 --> 00:02:52,539
When the war started back on February

43
00:02:52,539 --> 00:02:56,109
28th of this year,
the United States had four goals.

44
00:02:56,309 --> 00:03:00,346
Number one was regime change.

45
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:05,852
Number two was to put an end
to Iran's nuclear enrichment capability.

46
00:03:06,152 --> 00:03:11,824
Number three was to put an end
to Iran's long range missile capability.

47
00:03:11,824 --> 00:03:15,995
And number four 
was to end Iranian support

48
00:03:15,995 --> 00:03:18,932
for the Houthis, Hezbollah and Hamas.

49
00:03:18,932 --> 00:03:21,100
Those were the Big Four goals.

50
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:26,539
The United States, of course,
has achieved none of those goals.

51
00:03:26,573 --> 00:03:28,341
None of them have been achieved.

52
00:03:28,341 --> 00:03:33,479
And there is no chance that
any of the four are going to be achieved.

53
00:03:33,513 --> 00:03:36,716
So the question
we then have to ask ourselves is,

54
00:03:36,716 --> 00:03:40,253
what are America's
goals at this point in time?

55
00:03:40,553 --> 00:03:45,792
And Vice President Vance said last week
very clearly that we have two goals.

56
00:03:45,792 --> 00:03:49,862
He said that
the first and most important goal

57
00:03:49,929 --> 00:03:53,399
is to open the strait.

58
00:03:53,666 --> 00:03:58,471
He said the second goal
is to get a nuclear deal.

59
00:03:58,504 --> 00:04:03,076
This is actually quite remarkable
because when we went into the war,

60
00:04:03,076 --> 00:04:07,113
what mattered more than anything else
was the nuclear issue.

61
00:04:07,113 --> 00:04:12,018
And of course, the strait was opened on
February 27th.

62
00:04:12,018 --> 00:04:16,789
So the whole idea of opening
the strait was not even on the table

63
00:04:16,990 --> 00:04:19,359
right before the war started.

64
00:04:19,359 --> 00:04:21,928
It was the nuclear issue
that really mattered.

65
00:04:21,928 --> 00:04:27,734
So here's Vice President Vance
saying that our principal goal is

66
00:04:27,734 --> 00:04:31,070
to open the strait, and our secondary

67
00:04:31,104 --> 00:04:34,173
goal is to get a nuclear deal.

68
00:04:34,474 --> 00:04:39,712
By the way, President Trump's
spokesperson has said that

69
00:04:39,712 --> 00:04:43,983
as far as President Trump is concerned,
those two goals are co-equal.

70
00:04:43,983 --> 00:04:48,688
So he has a slightly different view
of things than Vice President Vance does.

71
00:04:48,688 --> 00:04:51,057
But nevertheless,
you can see how important it is

72
00:04:51,057 --> 00:04:54,894
for us to open the strait
as soon as possible.

73
00:04:54,894 --> 00:04:58,464
And that is obviously
for economic reasons.

74
00:04:58,665 --> 00:05:02,669
Now we want to do a switch gears,
and we want to talk about what

75
00:05:02,702 --> 00:05:04,137
Iran's goals are.

76
00:05:04,137 --> 00:05:08,274
And you want to keep in mind
what the United States wants to do

77
00:05:08,308 --> 00:05:12,578
is make sure that Iran does not achieve
any of its goals,

78
00:05:12,578 --> 00:05:17,517
if that's possible,
or all of its goals are denied.

79
00:05:17,517 --> 00:05:21,020
No, let me put it differently,
that we want to make sure that Iran

80
00:05:21,054 --> 00:05:24,157
doesn't achieve
all its all of its goals. And

81
00:05:25,458 --> 00:05:26,592
in the best case, to

82
00:05:26,592 --> 00:05:30,330
minimize the number of goals
that Iran achieves.

83
00:05:30,396 --> 00:05:34,267
Okay,
the Iranians do want to open the strait.

84
00:05:34,300 --> 00:05:35,535
There's no question about that.

85
00:05:35,535 --> 00:05:38,104
Like us,
they want to open the strait like us.

86
00:05:38,104 --> 00:05:41,574
They understand it's in their economic
interest to open the strait.

87
00:05:41,574 --> 00:05:47,213
But the Iranians say that they won't
open the strait unless the United States

88
00:05:47,714 --> 00:05:50,950
meets a number of other demands,

89
00:05:50,983 --> 00:05:54,020
not through negotiation, but right away.

90
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:57,623
In other words,
the Iranians say we'll open the strait

91
00:05:57,657 --> 00:06:02,228
if you agree to lift
all sanctions on Iran,

92
00:06:02,595 --> 00:06:05,531
if you agree to freeze

93
00:06:05,531 --> 00:06:10,069
all of unfreeze all of our frozen assets,

94
00:06:10,370 --> 00:06:14,340
if you agree to pay reparations,

95
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:17,910
if you agree that we control the strait,

96
00:06:17,910 --> 00:06:23,449
if you agree that we can collect fees,
if you agree to remove

97
00:06:23,449 --> 00:06:27,453
American military
forces from the Gulf region.

98
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:31,190
These are the demands
that the Iranians have.

99
00:06:31,224 --> 00:06:34,594
And the Iranians are saying
the United States has to meet

100
00:06:34,594 --> 00:06:37,930
all those demands
before they will open the strait.

101
00:06:38,197 --> 00:06:42,335
Now, obviously,
the United States is not interested

102
00:06:42,335 --> 00:06:45,905
in excepting all of those demands,

103
00:06:46,372 --> 00:06:51,310
but the Iranians have made it clear
that the United States has no choice.

104
00:06:51,411 --> 00:06:57,016
What we want to do is minimize the number
of demands that we are forced to accept.

105
00:06:57,049 --> 00:07:00,753
The Iranians say
there will be no minimization.

106
00:07:00,753 --> 00:07:06,826
You have to accept all of those demands
before we will agree to open the straight.

107
00:07:06,826 --> 00:07:09,061
And again,
you want to remember that America's

108
00:07:09,061 --> 00:07:12,832
principal goal is to open the strait. So

109
00:07:14,333 --> 00:07:15,301
the question is

110
00:07:15,301 --> 00:07:18,671
how can we achieve our goals?

111
00:07:19,138 --> 00:07:22,141
And.

112
00:07:22,475 --> 00:07:25,611
Blockade is the strategy of the day.

113
00:07:25,611 --> 00:07:29,182
But I want to say a few words
about what our strategy

114
00:07:29,182 --> 00:07:33,853
has been in the entire war,
so that we can put

115
00:07:33,853 --> 00:07:37,423
where we are today
with blockade into a broader context.

116
00:07:38,157 --> 00:07:43,262
As you remember,
the war started on February 28th,

117
00:07:43,262 --> 00:07:47,233
and we started with a major strategic
bombing campaign.

118
00:07:47,233 --> 00:07:50,503
An air campaign that lasted for 40 days

119
00:07:50,503 --> 00:07:53,773
went from February 20th 8th to April 8th.

120
00:07:54,106 --> 00:07:57,109
Then there was a cease fire on April 8th.

121
00:07:57,143 --> 00:08:00,079
There were some negotiations
that went nowhere.

122
00:08:00,079 --> 00:08:06,118
And then on April 13th, the United States
moved to a naval blockade.

123
00:08:06,152 --> 00:08:10,156
So you see,
this is the first time we tried blockade.

124
00:08:10,156 --> 00:08:14,861
And the blockade that is now in place
is the second instance of a blockade.

125
00:08:14,894 --> 00:08:20,500
So we went from an air war in the first
40 days of the war up till April 8th.

126
00:08:20,533 --> 00:08:23,970
Then on April 13th,
we moved to the first blockade.

127
00:08:23,970 --> 00:08:30,109
And that first blockade lasted from April
13th up until June

128
00:08:30,109 --> 00:08:33,346
17th, when the memorandum of understanding
was signed.

129
00:08:33,346 --> 00:08:38,384
So that's the way of saying
that the first blockade lasted

130
00:08:38,384 --> 00:08:44,090
about two plus months and it did not work.

131
00:08:44,190 --> 00:08:48,494
We ended up signing a memorandum
of understanding on June 17th.

132
00:08:48,494 --> 00:08:51,998
And that MOU, of course, is
what put an end to the blockade,

133
00:08:52,031 --> 00:08:53,232
the first blockade.

134
00:08:53,232 --> 00:09:00,373
But that memorandum of understanding
was extremely favorable to the Iranians.

135
00:09:00,373 --> 00:09:04,310
It was a memorandum of understanding
that the United States did not like,

136
00:09:04,310 --> 00:09:07,413
because the United States understood
it was a surrender document,

137
00:09:07,413 --> 00:09:10,116
but nevertheless,
the United States signed it

138
00:09:10,116 --> 00:09:13,553
and the United States signed it
because the blockade did not work.

139
00:09:13,553 --> 00:09:16,556
And before that, the air war did not work.

140
00:09:16,889 --> 00:09:21,093
Okay, then what happens in early July

141
00:09:21,260 --> 00:09:25,398
is that the United States and Iran

142
00:09:25,431 --> 00:09:29,635
get involved in a tit for tat strategy.

143
00:09:29,769 --> 00:09:35,341
This starts on July 7th
and it lasts until about July 31st.

144
00:09:35,341 --> 00:09:40,580
And it basically involves
the United States and Iran engaging

145
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:44,216
in a limited air war
where they bomb specific

146
00:09:44,250 --> 00:09:48,154
targets on each side.

147
00:09:48,187 --> 00:09:52,892
In other words, the United States bombs
targets in Iran and the Iranians bomb

148
00:09:52,959 --> 00:09:57,830
targets in Kuwait, Saudi Arabia
and so forth and so on.

149
00:09:57,830 --> 00:10:02,501
And this last, again
from July 7th to July 31st,

150
00:10:02,702 --> 00:10:05,972
then the tit for tat campaign

151
00:10:06,138 --> 00:10:09,008
comes to an end.

152
00:10:09,008 --> 00:10:13,779
And what happens is the United States

153
00:10:13,879 --> 00:10:17,450
moves to the second blockade.

154
00:10:17,516 --> 00:10:21,420
The second blockade
actually starts on July 14th,

155
00:10:21,420 --> 00:10:25,091
which is in the middle of the tit
for tat campaign.

156
00:10:25,091 --> 00:10:27,293
So there's a period there
where you have the tit

157
00:10:27,293 --> 00:10:31,330
for tat campaign taking place
and you have the second blockade.

158
00:10:31,330 --> 00:10:37,536
But as I said before, on July 31st,
the tit for tat campaign comes to an end.

159
00:10:37,570 --> 00:10:40,940
The United States
has basically had enough.

160
00:10:40,973 --> 00:10:45,411
So all that's left is the blockade,
the second blockade.

161
00:10:45,478 --> 00:10:48,481
And that's where we are today.

162
00:10:49,015 --> 00:10:52,351
Now, the question we have to ask ourselves

163
00:10:52,385 --> 00:10:57,056
is, given that the memorandum
of understanding has expired

164
00:10:57,056 --> 00:11:00,793
and there's no prospect
of a new memorandum of understanding.

165
00:11:00,860 --> 00:11:04,230
And given
that we've given up on tit for tat,

166
00:11:04,230 --> 00:11:08,534
given up on a major bombing campaign,
given that there's not going to be a land

167
00:11:08,601 --> 00:11:09,835
option.

168
00:11:09,835 --> 00:11:13,639
The question is, what will blockade do

169
00:11:13,673 --> 00:11:18,210
for the United States
in terms of achieving its objectives?

170
00:11:18,377 --> 00:11:21,614
Now, we know that the first blockade
didn't work.

171
00:11:21,614 --> 00:11:24,617
So the question is,
what about this second blockade?

172
00:11:24,650 --> 00:11:26,352
Now, it's important to start by saying

173
00:11:26,352 --> 00:11:29,455
that at the end of last week,
the Secretary of the Treasury,

174
00:11:29,455 --> 00:11:33,859
Scott Besson, said that this week,
starting today,

175
00:11:33,959 --> 00:11:39,031
we are going to inflict unprecedented
economic hurt

176
00:11:39,298 --> 00:11:43,536
on the Iranian economy.

177
00:11:43,536 --> 00:11:47,707
So, in effect, what he was saying
is that in addition to the blockade

178
00:11:47,707 --> 00:11:52,211
and this naval blockade is designed
to inflict economic hurt

179
00:11:52,578 --> 00:11:56,549
on the Iranian economy,
we're going to do something special

180
00:11:56,549 --> 00:12:02,788
this week, and we're going to deliver
unprecedented economic hurt. Now,

181
00:12:04,056 --> 00:12:04,423
let's

182
00:12:04,423 --> 00:12:07,893
evaluate
how well this is all likely to work.

183
00:12:08,260 --> 00:12:11,263
First of all, it's hard to believe

184
00:12:11,397 --> 00:12:17,903
that there's more economic hurt
that we can inflict on Iran.

185
00:12:17,937 --> 00:12:21,340
We've really maxed out at this point.

186
00:12:21,774 --> 00:12:26,579
What is the United States
going to do to damage further

187
00:12:26,612 --> 00:12:30,316
the Iranian economy
that it hasn't already done?

188
00:12:30,850 --> 00:12:34,353
Besson hasn't told us
he's made this vague claim,

189
00:12:34,353 --> 00:12:38,023
but he hasn't told us how this process
works.

190
00:12:38,190 --> 00:12:41,160
I would argue that this virtually nothing

191
00:12:41,160 --> 00:12:44,864
the United States can do to create

192
00:12:44,897 --> 00:12:48,200
unprecedented economic hurt.

193
00:12:48,734 --> 00:12:52,938
So this brings us
back to the question of the blockade.

194
00:12:53,539 --> 00:12:56,342
What is the likelihood that this blockade

195
00:12:56,342 --> 00:12:59,645
is going to produce a favorable outcome
for the United States?

196
00:12:59,678 --> 00:13:02,715
In other words, what is the likelihood
that this blockade is going

197
00:13:02,715 --> 00:13:07,119
to allow us to achieve our own goals
and limit

198
00:13:07,386 --> 00:13:12,191
the number of goals that Iran has
that it can achieve?

199
00:13:12,691 --> 00:13:17,530
Well, the first point you want to keep in
mind is that the first blockade failed.

200
00:13:17,897 --> 00:13:21,100
And if the first blockade failed,

201
00:13:21,267 --> 00:13:25,838
why should we expect the second blockade
to succeed?

202
00:13:26,105 --> 00:13:27,940
What's changed here?

203
00:13:27,940 --> 00:13:32,511
And by the way, you want to remember
that the first blockade, as I said, lasted

204
00:13:32,778 --> 00:13:37,383
a little over two months,
went from April 13th to June 17th.

205
00:13:37,383 --> 00:13:40,786
So a little over two months
and it didn't work again.

206
00:13:40,820 --> 00:13:42,788
We signed the memorandum of understanding,

207
00:13:42,788 --> 00:13:45,024
which was basically a surrender document.

208
00:13:45,024 --> 00:13:50,296
Well, the present blockade has gone on
since July 14th.

209
00:13:50,329 --> 00:13:53,432
It's a little over one month,
so we haven't even reached

210
00:13:53,432 --> 00:13:55,000
the two month period here.

211
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:59,572
And you want to keep in mind,
as I talk here, that blockades

212
00:13:59,572 --> 00:14:04,743
only work over a long period of time,
almost everybody agrees

213
00:14:04,743 --> 00:14:08,747
that blockades do not produce
instant results.

214
00:14:08,747 --> 00:14:12,618
So for this blockade to work,
it's going to have to be in place for

215
00:14:12,618 --> 00:14:14,119
a long period of time.

216
00:14:15,154 --> 00:14:18,224
We're not going to get instant results.

217
00:14:18,390 --> 00:14:24,430
Now, there are very good reasons
to think that we, the United States,

218
00:14:24,430 --> 00:14:29,134
are in a worse position today
in terms of implementing

219
00:14:29,134 --> 00:14:35,140
this blockade than we were when we started
the first blockade on April 13th.

220
00:14:35,174 --> 00:14:37,109
Now, why is that the case?

221
00:14:37,109 --> 00:14:40,279
One reason is the state of the US Navy.

222
00:14:40,312 --> 00:14:45,985
It's quite clear that the US Navy
is suffering in significant ways

223
00:14:45,985 --> 00:14:49,655
from the wear and tear of being deployed

224
00:14:49,755 --> 00:14:53,592
to the Gulf for a long period of time.

225
00:14:53,792 --> 00:14:57,496
And we see this with the case of the USS
Abraham Lincoln.

226
00:14:57,496 --> 00:15:01,500
This is the large aircraft carrier
where there has been

227
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:05,871
huge dissatisfaction
among the crew members

228
00:15:06,038 --> 00:15:09,141
because of the demands

229
00:15:09,141 --> 00:15:13,279
of this naval campaign against Iran.

230
00:15:13,312 --> 00:15:16,448
The United States has been forced
to deploy

231
00:15:17,016 --> 00:15:19,451
ships like this

232
00:15:19,451 --> 00:15:23,622
carrier, the Lincoln and destroyers,
and other ships as well,

233
00:15:23,789 --> 00:15:28,360
for extended periods of time in the area

234
00:15:28,360 --> 00:15:31,463
or in the waters around Iran.

235
00:15:31,463 --> 00:15:34,700
And those troops on

236
00:15:34,733 --> 00:15:39,338
these ships
have not been getting proper food,

237
00:15:39,405 --> 00:15:43,108
and they have not

238
00:15:43,108 --> 00:15:46,779
been in very good living conditions,

239
00:15:46,779 --> 00:15:52,584
and all sorts of trouble
has broken out on the Lincoln.

240
00:15:52,618 --> 00:15:56,822
There's evidence that some of the sailors
were thinking about

241
00:15:56,822 --> 00:16:01,961
jumping off the side of the carrier.

242
00:16:01,961 --> 00:16:07,499
There's evidence that fights had been
breaking out and so forth and so on.

243
00:16:07,499 --> 00:16:10,569
So actually what's happened is that the

244
00:16:10,569 --> 00:16:14,340
the Lincoln has been taken off station
and it's

245
00:16:14,340 --> 00:16:18,510
being replaced by another carrier
by the USS George Washington.

246
00:16:18,577 --> 00:16:22,982
Now, it's very important to emphasize
that it's not only the Lincoln

247
00:16:23,015 --> 00:16:24,950
that is suffering from these problems,

248
00:16:24,950 --> 00:16:28,420
destroyers and other ships
that have been deployed for extended

249
00:16:28,420 --> 00:16:34,193
periods of time are also having all sorts
of linguistic problems.

250
00:16:34,460 --> 00:16:37,896
So my point here is

251
00:16:38,063 --> 00:16:41,900
that for purposes of implementing

252
00:16:42,034 --> 00:16:47,072
a protracted blockade and blockades,
by definition of protracted,

253
00:16:47,139 --> 00:16:52,778
the US Navy is in terrible shape
to accomplish that mission.

254
00:16:53,312 --> 00:16:59,251
There's just been so much wear and tear
on the US Navy that it's not clear

255
00:16:59,318 --> 00:17:05,057
that it can keep in place a successful
blockade for many more months.

256
00:17:05,290 --> 00:17:10,062
There's an additional additional issue
at play here,

257
00:17:10,062 --> 00:17:13,932
and that is that

258
00:17:13,932 --> 00:17:17,302
the Navy, at this point in
time is small enough

259
00:17:17,369 --> 00:17:22,374
that it is only capable of focusing

260
00:17:22,374 --> 00:17:27,913
on one theater of operations
at a time, in a major way.

261
00:17:28,147 --> 00:17:33,385
And what this means is that
because we are focusing

262
00:17:33,385 --> 00:17:38,490
laser like on the Middle East
or on the Persian Gulf or on Iran,

263
00:17:38,857 --> 00:17:45,297
what we are doing
is weakening ourselves in East Asia.

264
00:17:45,297 --> 00:17:49,301
And you want to remember that
in East Asia, where the United States

265
00:17:49,334 --> 00:17:55,074
is interested in containing China,
naval forces matter greatly.

266
00:17:55,107 --> 00:17:59,611
Naval and air forces matter
much more than land forces in East Asia.

267
00:17:59,611 --> 00:18:02,414
When you talk about containing China.

268
00:18:02,414 --> 00:18:06,351
But what's happening here
is that the United States is being forced

269
00:18:06,351 --> 00:18:11,623
to pull forces out of East Asia
and send them to the Persian Gulf.

270
00:18:11,623 --> 00:18:14,093
And this is true
with regard to naval forces.

271
00:18:14,093 --> 00:18:18,997
As I said before, given the consequences
of the logistical problems

272
00:18:18,997 --> 00:18:23,268
that the Abraham Lincoln
faces, it's being taken off station

273
00:18:23,268 --> 00:18:27,372
and it's being replaced,
as I said, by the US as George Washington.

274
00:18:27,439 --> 00:18:28,040
The question

275
00:18:28,040 --> 00:18:31,777
you want to ask yourself is, where is
the George Washington coming from?

276
00:18:31,777 --> 00:18:35,114
And the fact is, it's coming from East
Asia.

277
00:18:35,147 --> 00:18:38,383
It's normally stationed in Japan.

278
00:18:38,517 --> 00:18:43,889
This means there's going to be no aircraft
carrier in East Asia.

279
00:18:44,189 --> 00:18:48,760
This is not good
for a strategic point of view.

280
00:18:48,794 --> 00:18:52,131
When you think about containing China.

281
00:18:52,231 --> 00:18:57,302
So not only is the wear and tear on the US

282
00:18:57,302 --> 00:19:01,673
Navy limiting its overall capabilities,

283
00:19:02,674 --> 00:19:05,677
the focus on Iran

284
00:19:05,744 --> 00:19:10,349
keeping a blockade on
Iran is also weakening America's position

285
00:19:10,349 --> 00:19:16,121
in East Asia vis a vis
its most important threat, which is China.

286
00:19:17,089 --> 00:19:20,659
There are additional problems
with the blockade.

287
00:19:21,226 --> 00:19:26,098
One is that as long as we keep
the blockade on and the Iranians

288
00:19:26,131 --> 00:19:31,036
keep the blockade
on, what we are doing is greatly limiting

289
00:19:31,036 --> 00:19:36,141
the amount of oil
that comes out of the Persian Gulf,

290
00:19:36,208 --> 00:19:41,013
because both sides are, in effect,
blockading the Strait of Hormuz.

291
00:19:41,079 --> 00:19:46,585
This means that there's less oil to appear

292
00:19:46,585 --> 00:19:50,222
on global or in global markets.

293
00:19:50,255 --> 00:19:53,825
Furthermore,
because there has been a huge conflict

294
00:19:54,226 --> 00:19:57,129
between the Houthis and the Saudis,

295
00:19:57,129 --> 00:20:02,801
and the Houthis
have basically shut off the Bab El

296
00:20:02,834 --> 00:20:07,539
Mondavi Strait, which is where Saudi oil
comes out of the Red sea.

297
00:20:08,373 --> 00:20:12,010
Saudi oil is now not only coming out

298
00:20:12,978 --> 00:20:16,782
of the
Persian Gulf through the Strait of Hormuz.

299
00:20:16,782 --> 00:20:21,920
It's not coming
out of the Red sea through El Mundo.

300
00:20:21,920 --> 00:20:25,657
And it is imperative
to have lots of Saudi oil

301
00:20:25,857 --> 00:20:28,827
on our in global markets.

302
00:20:28,827 --> 00:20:31,063
But that's no longer the case.

303
00:20:31,063 --> 00:20:34,933
This is not to deny,
of course, that some ships do get through

304
00:20:35,267 --> 00:20:39,538
either the Strait of Hormuz
or the Bab El Mundo Strait.

305
00:20:39,538 --> 00:20:42,074
Some sortie ships do, but not many.

306
00:20:42,074 --> 00:20:45,077
So the amount of Saudi oil that was coming

307
00:20:45,077 --> 00:20:50,849
out of the Gulf on February 27th
has been greatly reduced,

308
00:20:50,916 --> 00:20:56,054
and this is a huge problem
for the international economy.

309
00:20:56,388 --> 00:21:00,459
So there are all sorts of economic

310
00:21:00,559 --> 00:21:05,097
incentives to open the strait.

311
00:21:05,097 --> 00:21:07,132
I might also note that

312
00:21:07,132 --> 00:21:11,336
if you look at the Strategic Petroleum
Reserve in the United States

313
00:21:11,336 --> 00:21:16,041
and the Strategic Petroleum Reserve
in other countries, it's quite clear

314
00:21:16,041 --> 00:21:19,177
that we have significantly drawn down

315
00:21:19,177 --> 00:21:24,349
those reserves to make up for the fact

316
00:21:24,349 --> 00:21:29,388
that so much oil is now not coming
out of the Gulf.

317
00:21:29,621 --> 00:21:33,992
And the question is,
how long can we depend on

318
00:21:34,092 --> 00:21:38,397
taking all out of those strategic
petroleum reserve in places

319
00:21:38,397 --> 00:21:43,302
like the United States
to deal with this problem of,

320
00:21:44,369 --> 00:21:46,471
of of

321
00:21:46,471 --> 00:21:50,409
missing or coming out of the Persian
Gulf.

322
00:21:50,542 --> 00:21:55,614
Now, another
very important dimension to this

323
00:21:55,614 --> 00:22:00,919
is that if you look at what's happened
inside of Iran,

324
00:22:01,620 --> 00:22:04,623
the hardliners are now in control

325
00:22:05,257 --> 00:22:09,294
and the hard liners are not interested

326
00:22:09,294 --> 00:22:13,265
in reaching any kind of accommodation
with the United States.

327
00:22:13,298 --> 00:22:16,201
They are interested in driving a hard
bargain.

328
00:22:16,201 --> 00:22:19,538
They interest they are interested
in telling the United States

329
00:22:19,571 --> 00:22:26,678
it has to meet all of Iran's demands,
which I described before.

330
00:22:26,812 --> 00:22:29,948
Before Iran will agree to open

331
00:22:30,315 --> 00:22:32,951
the Strait of Hormuz.

332
00:22:32,951 --> 00:22:37,422
And in addition to that, these hardliners

333
00:22:37,422 --> 00:22:40,659
who are in charge inside of Iran,

334
00:22:40,692 --> 00:22:43,695
or making it perfectly clear

335
00:22:43,829 --> 00:22:46,631
that they want to

336
00:22:46,631 --> 00:22:50,669
initiate military action

337
00:22:50,702 --> 00:22:54,940
against the United States
and its allies in the Persian Gulf.

338
00:22:54,973 --> 00:22:59,478
They believe that the best defense
is a good offense.

339
00:22:59,511 --> 00:23:04,783
So if you look at the hardliners
who are now in control in Iran,

340
00:23:05,183 --> 00:23:08,820
they are bent on causing

341
00:23:08,820 --> 00:23:12,057
the United States more trouble than ever.

342
00:23:12,090 --> 00:23:15,293
They are interested
in taking the initiative.

343
00:23:15,494 --> 00:23:18,263
They are interested in being proactive.

344
00:23:18,263 --> 00:23:24,369
So the United States now
faces a more dangerous adversary in Iran.

345
00:23:24,503 --> 00:23:28,373
In addition
to the fact that the economic situation

346
00:23:28,373 --> 00:23:33,578
is deteriorating for the reasons
I just described, and in addition

347
00:23:33,578 --> 00:23:38,617
to the fact that the US
Navy is in bad shape

348
00:23:39,317 --> 00:23:42,454
for purposes of implementing

349
00:23:42,454 --> 00:23:46,792
a protracted naval blockade of Iran.

350
00:23:46,825 --> 00:23:50,729
So the situation does not look good
at all.

351
00:23:50,796 --> 00:23:55,267
Now, one might say, okay,
you've convinced me

352
00:23:55,267 --> 00:24:00,539
that the blockade is not likely to work,
but maybe with the United States can do

353
00:24:00,572 --> 00:24:04,009
here is go back to a tit
for tit for tat strategy.

354
00:24:04,075 --> 00:24:07,345
Or maybe the United States can go back

355
00:24:07,345 --> 00:24:11,550
to a major bombing campaign
like we saw in the first 40 days.

356
00:24:11,583 --> 00:24:15,353
In other words,
we do have an escalation option.

357
00:24:15,554 --> 00:24:17,289
But this is not true.

358
00:24:17,289 --> 00:24:19,958
We don't have an escalation option.

359
00:24:19,958 --> 00:24:24,863
In fact, we don't even want to go back
to a tit for tat strategy.

360
00:24:24,863 --> 00:24:27,866
And there are basically three reasons
for that.

361
00:24:28,033 --> 00:24:32,771
One is we have a significant shortage
of munitions.

362
00:24:32,771 --> 00:24:38,543
And the last thing we want to do
is get involved in a tit for tat campaign

363
00:24:38,577 --> 00:24:42,380
where we have to use up
even more of those precious munitions.

364
00:24:42,614 --> 00:24:47,986
Furthermore, our Gulf allies
have zero interest in seeing the war start

365
00:24:47,986 --> 00:24:53,625
back up again because they understand full
well that they will be targeted.

366
00:24:53,859 --> 00:24:55,393
The Iranians have made that clear.

367
00:24:55,393 --> 00:24:59,998
And of course, in the past the Iranians
have targeted our Gulf allies.

368
00:25:00,065 --> 00:25:03,702
So our Gulf allies are saying
no more escalation.

369
00:25:03,702 --> 00:25:06,538
And then finally,
there's the economic consequences.

370
00:25:06,538 --> 00:25:10,609
At this point in time,
President Trump cannot afford

371
00:25:10,609 --> 00:25:16,848
to restart the 40 day bombing campaign
or even engage in a tit for tat campaign.

372
00:25:16,848 --> 00:25:18,550
He's got to open the strait.

373
00:25:18,550 --> 00:25:23,221
That's why Vice President Vance said,
our principal goal is to open the strait.

374
00:25:23,455 --> 00:25:27,292
We have to open that straight
for economic reasons.

375
00:25:27,292 --> 00:25:31,096
So tit for tat is not even a viable option

376
00:25:31,096 --> 00:25:34,866
for the United States,
much less starting the 40 day campaign.

377
00:25:34,866 --> 00:25:37,736
And to drive that point home.

378
00:25:37,736 --> 00:25:42,107
Let me tell you a story that shows you 
how little interest

379
00:25:42,107 --> 00:25:45,977
we have, even in escalating
to a tit for tat campaign.

380
00:25:46,945 --> 00:25:50,015
As I said before, the tit for tat campaign

381
00:25:50,015 --> 00:25:54,019
started on July 7th
and it lasted until July 31st.

382
00:25:54,052 --> 00:25:57,656
Remember we had first
the strategic bombing campaign.

383
00:25:57,689 --> 00:26:01,493
Then we had the first naval blockade
and we had the tit for tat campaign.

384
00:26:01,493 --> 00:26:03,995
It started on July 7th.

385
00:26:03,995 --> 00:26:05,931
Now why did it start?

386
00:26:05,931 --> 00:26:10,235
How did the
how did the tit for tat campaign begin

387
00:26:10,902 --> 00:26:13,505
on July 6th,

388
00:26:13,505 --> 00:26:17,342
the Iranians attacked three

389
00:26:18,610 --> 00:26:23,381
cargo vessels
that were trying to come out of the Strait

390
00:26:23,381 --> 00:26:27,052
of Hormuz through the Omani Channel.

391
00:26:27,118 --> 00:26:30,221
That was the channel
that's close to Amman that

392
00:26:30,221 --> 00:26:33,692
the United States,
in effect, helped control.

393
00:26:34,192 --> 00:26:37,963
The Iranians made it clear to us
that that was unacceptable.

394
00:26:37,996 --> 00:26:42,000
No ships coming out of the Gulf
could go through

395
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:45,537
the Omani channel, said the Iranians.

396
00:26:45,537 --> 00:26:50,976
Well, on July 6th, there were three ships
coming through the Omani channel

397
00:26:50,976 --> 00:26:55,213
in the Strait of Hormuz,
and the Iranians opened

398
00:26:55,280 --> 00:26:58,350
fire on all three of those ships.

399
00:26:58,383 --> 00:27:00,018
This is July 6th.

400
00:27:00,018 --> 00:27:03,888
The next day,
the United States started the tit

401
00:27:03,888 --> 00:27:09,094
for tat campaign
by attacking targets inside of Iran.

402
00:27:09,160 --> 00:27:11,496
The Iranians, of course, retaliated.

403
00:27:11,496 --> 00:27:14,332
We retaliated and so forth and so on.

404
00:27:14,332 --> 00:27:19,704
And this tit for tat campaign lasted
from the 7th of July to the 31st of July.

405
00:27:19,838 --> 00:27:23,742
The key point that I want to make here
is what started it was that

406
00:27:23,742 --> 00:27:28,813
the Iranians attacked cargo ships
going through the Strait of Hormuz,

407
00:27:28,813 --> 00:27:32,784
and the United States said
that is categorically unacceptable.

408
00:27:32,784 --> 00:27:35,620
And therefore
we went to the tit for tat campaign.

409
00:27:35,620 --> 00:27:39,057
Well,
if you look at what's happening today,

410
00:27:39,491 --> 00:27:43,428
the Iranians are attacking

411
00:27:43,628 --> 00:27:46,698
cargo ships in the Strait

412
00:27:46,698 --> 00:27:50,702
of Hormuz on an almost daily basis.

413
00:27:50,735 --> 00:27:52,937
They make no bones about it.

414
00:27:52,937 --> 00:27:57,342
And the question you want to ask yourself
is, what is the United States doing?

415
00:27:57,342 --> 00:28:00,178
Is the United States retaliating?

416
00:28:00,178 --> 00:28:04,649
Is the United States shown any interest
in going back to a tip for tat strategy?

417
00:28:04,716 --> 00:28:06,051
The answer is no.

418
00:28:06,051 --> 00:28:10,855
And the reason is that the United States
has zero

419
00:28:10,855 --> 00:28:15,193
interest
in escalating to a tit for tat strategy.

420
00:28:15,593 --> 00:28:19,064
So what we have
here is a very interesting situation.

421
00:28:19,064 --> 00:28:22,867
If you think about it, if escalation

422
00:28:22,867 --> 00:28:28,640
does take place in the Gulf, it's
not the United States

423
00:28:28,640 --> 00:28:33,411
that is likely to initiate
that escalation.

424
00:28:33,411 --> 00:28:37,215
It is the Iranians who are likely

425
00:28:37,215 --> 00:28:40,752
to initiate that escalation,

426
00:28:40,752 --> 00:28:44,389
because the leadership in Iran

427
00:28:44,422 --> 00:28:48,159
very clearly now is interested in playing

428
00:28:48,159 --> 00:28:52,197
hardball with the United States
from a military perspective.

429
00:28:52,230 --> 00:28:56,234
As I said before, from
an Iranian point of view, they believe

430
00:28:56,234 --> 00:29:02,107
that the best defense is a good offense,
so they are willing to escalate

431
00:29:02,107 --> 00:29:06,411
because they believe
that the United States will not retaliate.

432
00:29:06,478 --> 00:29:09,681
Now, it is possible
the United States will retaliate

433
00:29:09,681 --> 00:29:13,418
at some point,
but that will not solve our problem.

434
00:29:13,852 --> 00:29:18,456
So you see, what's happened here
is that over time,

435
00:29:19,090 --> 00:29:21,793
the United States has failed

436
00:29:21,793 --> 00:29:26,331
to achieve any of its objectives,
whether we're talking about the four

437
00:29:26,331 --> 00:29:31,336
big objectives that had started the war
with or opening the strait

438
00:29:31,836 --> 00:29:36,775
or getting a nuclear deal,
which are its two objectives today.

439
00:29:36,808 --> 00:29:40,478
And at the same time, it's in a situation

440
00:29:40,478 --> 00:29:44,215
where it looks like it has little choice

441
00:29:44,349 --> 00:29:49,754
but to cave in to Iran's major demands,

442
00:29:49,988 --> 00:29:53,825
because the United States has no strategy,

443
00:29:53,825 --> 00:29:59,330
it has no military option
for achieving its goals

444
00:29:59,330 --> 00:30:04,135
and preventing Iran from gaining
its goals.

445
00:30:04,169 --> 00:30:07,272
It's been forced into a situation

446
00:30:07,272 --> 00:30:10,508
where it has to rely on blockade.

447
00:30:10,542 --> 00:30:15,680
And as I said before, blockade didn't work
the first time, and there's no reason

448
00:30:15,680 --> 00:30:20,185
to think it's going to work this time,
because the situation

449
00:30:20,185 --> 00:30:23,388
for the United States,
in terms of implementing a blockade

450
00:30:23,421 --> 00:30:26,691
now is worse than it was between April

451
00:30:26,691 --> 00:30:29,794
13th and June 17th.

452
00:30:29,994 --> 00:30:32,997
And if you think about it,
what's happened here

453
00:30:33,464 --> 00:30:37,735
is that the Iranians have really turned

454
00:30:37,769 --> 00:30:41,739
the table tables in the sense

455
00:30:41,840 --> 00:30:46,010
that the Iranians are now

456
00:30:46,578 --> 00:30:53,451
the party in this conflict
that is most likely to cause escalation

457
00:30:53,518 --> 00:30:58,189
in the beginning, that obviously
looked like the United States.

458
00:30:58,189 --> 00:31:02,594
It looked in the very beginning,
like the United States held all the cards.

459
00:31:02,660 --> 00:31:06,064
But as this conflict has worn on,

460
00:31:06,064 --> 00:31:12,070
and as we've come to understand
here on August 17th,

461
00:31:12,303 --> 00:31:15,139
we have no military option.

462
00:31:15,139 --> 00:31:18,209
Escalation would be disastrous for us.

463
00:31:18,209 --> 00:31:22,914
And if anything, it's the Iranians
who believe

464
00:31:22,947 --> 00:31:29,153
they have a military option
and they believe they can escalate.

465
00:31:29,320 --> 00:31:32,624
All of this is to say that it's hard

466
00:31:32,624 --> 00:31:38,129
to see how the United States can achieve

467
00:31:38,196 --> 00:31:42,800
even a limited victory
in this conflict with Iran.

468
00:31:42,834 --> 00:31:47,105
It looks to me here on August 17th,

469
00:31:47,138 --> 00:31:52,744
like the United States has little choice
but to surrender

470
00:31:52,744 --> 00:31:57,615
to virtually all of Iran's demands

471
00:31:57,615 --> 00:32:02,720
if it hopes to open the strait
and avoid an economic catastrophe.

472
00:32:03,254 --> 00:32:07,492
That concludes my video for today.

473
00:32:07,492 --> 00:32:11,796
Thank you all for listening,
and I look forward

474
00:32:11,796 --> 00:32:14,899
to the next time that we talk.

